    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The genotypic value of genotype $ B_{i}B_{j} $ is a function of $ \\mu_{G} $, the mean genotypic value in the population, $ \\alpha_{1} $ and $ \\alpha_{2} $, the slopes of the regression, $ N_{1} $ and $ N_{2} $, the predictor variables, and $ \\delta_{ij} $, the residual error. This partitioning of genotypic values into various components is one of several major advances developed in Fisher's 1918 paper. Many of the innovative ideas in this classic paper are presented in a characteristically cursory manner, but a useful interpretative guide has been produced by Moran and Smith (1966).",
      "source_page": 81,
      "printed_page": 61,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter4:p015:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
